      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2749.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 649,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Weird and Wonderful Molecule)\nWater is one of the most unique and unusual substances in the universe. \n* **Three States of Matter:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states: **solid** (ice), **liquid** (water), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is often called the \"universal solvent.\" It carries nutrients, minerals, and chemicals wherever it goes.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water does the opposite; when it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater is the defining feature of our home planet. \n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Only about **2.5%** of Earth's water is fresh water. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through a closed system called the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from the oceans, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the sea.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it is entirely dependent on water.\n* **Biological Necessity:** The human body is made of about **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. We need it to regulate body temperature, flush out waste, lubricate joints, and transport nutrients through our blood.\n* **The Cradle of Evolution:** Scientists believe that life itself originated in the watery depths of Earth's early oceans billions of years ago. \n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes our geography, drives our weather systems, and is the literal lifeblood of every living organism on Earth."
